      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright 2004 Pathological Society of Great Britain and Ireland",
        "AbstractText": "The major limitation of percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) is restenosis. Restenosis is considered to be an overreaction of the natural healing process after traumatic balloon dilatation. An elaborate web of cellular and molecular responses, including the interaction of platelets, leukocytes, and the coagulation-fibrinolysis system, as well as the secretion of various growth factors and pro-inflammatory cytokines, contributes to neointimal hyperplasia and the development of restenosis. Moreover, platelet and neutrophil activation after stenting appears to be different from that after balloon angioplasty alone. Pharmacotherapy targeting the cell-to-cell interaction between platelets and neutrophils may potentially offer an effective treatment strategy against restenosis after PCI."
      },
